#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ccrc-wrapper-shape — THE ACCOUNT WRAPPER CONTRACT, in one file: what a
# generated wrapper looks like on disk, how to read one without ever reading a
# 300 MB binary whole, and the both-directions set difference its callers use to
# report a roster and a disk that disagree.
#
# SOURCED, never executed. Two callers today, asking the same question from
# opposite ends:
#   - `ccd/ccrc-adopt` — disk -> roster. It classifies what it finds in
#     $HOME/.local/bin and writes ~/.ccrc/accounts.json for a box nobody
#     generated one for yet.
#   - `ccd/ccrc-doctor-checks`'s `_check_wrappers` — roster vs disk. It reports
#     where the two disagree, and resolves nothing.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Both callers need the SAME answer to "what does a generated wrapper look
# like", and a second, looser copy of that answer is precisely how a checker
# and a generator drift apart — which would make the checker worse than
# useless: it would go green on the shape it had learned to accept rather than
# on the shape the tools actually write. This is the same rule
# `server/test/single-definition.test.ts` enforces for TypeScript, applied to
# the two bash tools that share one contract. `parse_shape` and the set
# difference below were MOVED here out of `ccrc-adopt`; neither was rewritten.
#
# ── THE THIRD SHIPPED FILE (read this before installing anything) ─────────
# `ccrc` finds `ccrc-doctor-checks` through `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`, and this file
# the same way. Bash does NOT resolve `BASH_SOURCE` through a symlink, so a
# symlink to `ccrc` on PATH does not rescue a missing sibling: DOCTOR's three
# files — `ccrc`, `ccrc-doctor-checks` and this one — have to be installed into
# one directory together. (The CLI's own set is FOUR: `ccrc-adopt` as well,
# which `cmd_adopt` execs. Doctor does not need it, which is why this paragraph
# counts three and `ccrc`'s header counts four; they are counting different
# sets, not disagreeing.) `_check_wrappers` says so by name when this file is
# not beside it, and `ccrc-doctor.test.ts` pins that message — a red suite
# rather than a comment.
#
# ── NAMESPACE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything here is `_wrap_*` / `WRAPPER_*`, which is neither of the two
# namespaces already in play in a process that sources this file:
#   - `_ccrc_*`  — the CLI's helpers (`_ccrc_die`) AND, more importantly, the
#     five functions `shared/generate.mjs` emits into ~/.ccrc/accounts.sh
#     (`_ccrc_cfg_dir`, `_ccrc_id_wrapper`, `_ccrc_dir_id`, `_ccrc_label`,
#     `_ccrc_hue`) plus `CCRC_ACCOUNTS`/`CCRC_HOME_ABLE`/`CCRC_MEASURED`/
#     `CCRC_UPSTREAM`. Nothing here may shadow one of those: a doctor run that
#     redefined `_ccrc_cfg_dir` would be lying to whatever sourced it next.
#   - `_dr_*`    — `ccrc-doctor-checks`'s own verdict helpers.
#
# ── NO PATH LOOKUPS, DELIBERATELY ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Not one function here calls an external binary — no `head`, no `grep`, no
# `readlink`, no `basename`. Two reasons, both measured:
#   - doctor's whole job is to answer on a box whose environment is broken, and
#     `ccrc-doctor.test.ts` runs it under a PATH containing nothing but fixture
#     directories (no /bin, no /usr/bin). A check that needs coreutils to
#     classify a file goes silent on exactly the box it exists for.
#   - `adopt`'s "never read a candidate whole" rule (its own header) is kept
#     here by `_wrap_is_script` reading exactly two bytes. Measured on the real
#     324 MB `claude` binary: instant, and nothing but those two bytes is read.

# Where account wrappers live. One spelling, because two files comparing
# "$HOME/.local/bin" against each other is the same drift in miniature.
WRAPPER_BIN_DIR="$HOME/.local/bin"

# `shared/roster.ts`'s ID_RE. A wrapper's FILENAME is its account id — ccd
# executes `$HOME/.local/bin/<id>` directly (ccd:7361) — so this doubles as the
# filter that drops the `ccd.bak-<date>` / `claude2.bak-<date>` backups a real
# box accumulates: a "." never appears in a legal id.
WRAPPER_ID_RE='^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}$'
# Mirrors `shared/roster.ts`'s second, independent gate on configDirSuffix: the
# safe set a suffix may use once its leading "." is counted. Kept here, not
# derived from the TypeScript, for the same reason `shared/roster-json.mjs`
# keeps its own copy (see that file's header) — a bare bash script cannot
# import shared/roster.ts either.
WRAPPER_SUFFIX_SAFE_RE='^\.[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$'

# D-155. The size past which a file is not classified AT ALL — the bash-side
# spelling of `deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs`'s `OVERSIZE_BYTES` (D-81), and the
# reason it is a constant here rather than a literal at each call site: it was
# already written out twice in bash (`ccrc-doctor-checks`, bare `1048576`) and
# the gate change below needed a third. Two spellings remain, one per language,
# and that is the floor — a bare `node` cannot import bash and a bare bash
# cannot import the ESM.
#
# THE COMPARISON, NOT THE READ, LIVES AT THE CALL SITES. Measuring a file needs
# `stat`, and this file makes no PATH lookups (see the header) — so what ships
# here is the number, and each caller does its own `stat` in whatever guarded
# idiom it already uses (`ccrc-adopt` calls `stat` outright, as it already does
# for its own reporting; `ccrc-doctor-checks` guards with `command -v`, as it
# does for every external tool). The two callers also want OPPOSITE fail-safe
# directions when a size cannot be had, which is the second reason this is a
# number and not a predicate: adopt REFUSES an unmeasurable upstream, while the
# candidate scan SKIPS an unmeasurable candidate. One predicate answering both
# would have to fold those into one boolean, and that is the overloaded-null
# defect this codebase treats as a bug rather than a style.
WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES=1048576

# D-156. A SECOND, TIGHTER threshold, and deliberately not a re-spelling of the
# one above. `WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES` answers "is this too big to be judged at
# all"; this answers "is this too big to be a GENERATED WRAPPER", which is a far
# smaller claim and provable from the shape itself: `_wrap_parse_shape` accepts
# two or three significant lines, and a real generated wrapper measures 370-374
# bytes on the reference box. 64 KiB is ~175x headroom.
#
# It exists because the witness scan in `ccrc wrappers` reads EVERY id-shaped
# file in ~/.local/bin, not just the ones the roster names. Measured on the real
# 86-entry bin dir: 0.15 s at 64 KiB against 0.70 s at 1 MiB, for the IDENTICAL
# witness set. The whole 0.55 s difference is one file — `~/.local/bin/ccd`,
# 608 KB, whose first line really is this shebang and whose second line really
# does carry a ccrc marker, so no cheaper filter drops it.
#
# The tightness also turns the cap from a hole into an entailment: under the
# strict "a witness must parse as the full generated shape" rule, a file over
# this size is PROVABLY not a witness, so skipping it unread loses nothing. A
# looser "does the text contain an exec line" rule would have made the same cap
# a silent blind spot.
WRAPPER_WITNESS_MAX_BYTES=65536

# The generated shape's first line, spelled once. `_wrap_parse_shape` compares
# against it, and the witness scan uses it as a cheap pre-filter so it can drop
# a candidate before paying for a process substitution — measured, that gate
# turns 66 parses into 15 on a live-shaped bin dir.
WRAPPER_SHEBANG='#!/usr/bin/env bash'


# ── is this file a script? ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The first two bytes, and never a third. `read -N 2` reads EXACTLY two
# characters and stops, delimiters and all — the file's remaining 324 MB are
# never touched. (`-n` would stop early at a newline; `-N` does not, which is
# what makes this a byte test rather than a line test.) Symlinks are followed
# transparently, because this opens the file for reading rather than `stat`ing
# the link — the measured shape on the reference box is `claude` ->
# ~/.local/share/claude/versions/<ver>, whose own `stat` size is the length of
# that path string.
_wrap_is_script() {
  local first=""
  [ -f "$1" ] && [ -r "$1" ] || return 1
  IFS= read -r -N 2 first 2>/dev/null < "$1" || true
  [ "$first" = '#!' ]
}

# ── does this script declare a config dir at all? ─────────────────────────
# The one question that separates "an account wrapper" from "some other script
# in ~/.local/bin" — and, since D-155, the question `ccrc-adopt` asks of the
# upstream account it just elected, because "is it a script" stopped being a
# proxy for it the day the binary path became a launcher.
#
# TWO OBLIGATIONS ON THE CALLER, and this function enforces neither, because it
# cannot: it makes no PATH lookups and so cannot measure anything.
#   1. Call it only on something `_wrap_is_script` accepted — on a non-script
#      this reads the whole file.
#   2. Size it first, against `WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES`. `_wrap_is_script`'s
#      two-byte bound says nothing about how big a SCRIPT is, and this reads
#      whatever it is handed to the last line before answering `1`. Both
#      callers do it; each picks its own direction for a size it cannot get
#      (`ccrc-adopt` refuses, its candidate scan skips).
#
# The answer is a two-state one on purpose — declares, or does not. "Could not
# tell" is deliberately NOT folded in as a third `return`: the two callers
# resolve that case differently, and a predicate that collapsed it would be
# handing them one value for two conditions they must not treat alike.
_wrap_declares_config_dir() {
  local f="$1" ln
  [ -r "$f" ] || return 1
  while IFS= read -r ln; do
    [[ "$ln" =~ ^[[:space:]]*export[[:space:]]+CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR= ]] && return 0
  done < "$f"
  return 1
}

# ── the generated shape ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Target-agnostic, and deliberately ONE function used for several different
# questions (which binary is upstream; which scripts are "generated"; whether a
# roster entry still describes what is on disk), because every one of them
# depends on the exact same shape test and a second, looser copy of it is how
# they would drift.
#
# "Full shape match" means: the shebang line is EXACTLY `#!/usr/bin/env bash`;
# every remaining blank or comment-only line is stripped; what is left is
# EXACTLY two or three lines, in order — an export, an optional
# secrets-sourcing guard, and an exec of SOME local binary — with nothing else.
# Comments are the one thing this tolerates ANYWHERE in that gap: the two real
# generated wrappers measured on the reference box (claude2, claude-dev0) both
# carry an explanatory comment ahead of their secrets line, and a classifier
# that could not round-trip its own prior output would be useless.
#
# The exec line's TARGET is captured but not judged here — that is the caller's
# question, and it differs per caller (adopt holds a vote; doctor compares the
# target against the roster's upstream account). A script with the right shape
# but the wrong target must never be judged by this function, which is what
# resolving the target here would risk.
#
# EACH SIGNIFICANT LINE IS CHECKED BY STRIPPING the known literal prefix and
# suffix (quoted, so no part of it is ever treated as a glob or regex) and then
# RECONSTRUCTING the exact line this file expects from what was captured,
# comparing the two strings whole. That is deliberate, and it is the reason
# `_check_wrappers` can exist at all: a regex capturing two occurrences of the
# same path in one line is exactly the kind of pattern that is easy to write
# wrong in a way that silently accepts a MISMATCHED pair — which is the very
# defect class the doctor check hunts. Reconstruct-then-compare cannot make
# that mistake: it comes back with the reconstructed answer, or the line
# disagrees.
#
# Prints `ok\t<target>\t<suffix>\t<secrets>` or `no\t\t\t`. Both the suffix and
# the secrets path are HOME-RELATIVE, exactly as the roster spells them.
#
# THE SECRETS FILE IS A PATH AND NOTHING ELSE. This reads the WRAPPER; it never
# opens, stats, sources or hashes the file the wrapper names. A token that is
# never read cannot be printed, and doctor's output is the thing an operator
# pastes into a ticket.
_wrap_parse_shape() {
  local f="$1"
  local -a lines=()
  [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return; }
  mapfile -t lines < "$f"

  if [ "${lines[0]:-}" != "$WRAPPER_SHEBANG" ]; then
    printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return
  fi

  local -a body=()
  local i ln
  for ((i = 1; i < ${#lines[@]}; i++)); do
    ln="${lines[$i]}"
    [[ "$ln" =~ ^[[:space:]]*$ ]] && continue
    [[ "$ln" =~ ^[[:space:]]*# ]] && continue
    body+=("$ln")
  done

  local n="${#body[@]}"
  if [ "$n" -ne 2 ] && [ "$n" -ne 3 ]; then
    printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return
  fi

  # Line 1: export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/<suffix>"
  local exp_prefix='export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/'
  ln="${body[0]}"
  if [[ "$ln" != "$exp_prefix"* || "$ln" != *'"' ]]; then
    printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return
  fi
  # exp_prefix ends in "/", one character short of the suffix's own leading
  # "." (the file reads `$HOME/.claude-personal`) — so `suffix` already
  # starts with "." here. Do not prepend a second one.
  local suffix="${ln#"$exp_prefix"}"
  suffix="${suffix%\"}"
  if [ "$suffix" = "." ] || [[ ! "$suffix" =~ $WRAPPER_SUFFIX_SAFE_RE ]]; then
    printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return
  fi

  local secrets=""
  local idx=1
  if [ "$n" -eq 3 ]; then
    local sec_prefix='[ -r "$HOME/'
    ln="${body[1]}"
    if [[ "$ln" != "$sec_prefix"*'"'* ]]; then
      printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return
    fi
    local rest="${ln#"$sec_prefix"}"
    local path1="${rest%%\"*}"
    local expected="$sec_prefix$path1"'" ] && . "$HOME/'"$path1"'"'
    if [ "$ln" != "$expected" ]; then
      printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return
    fi
    secrets="$path1"
    idx=2
  fi

  # exec "$HOME/.local/bin/<target>" "$@" — target not judged here.
  local exec_prefix='exec "$HOME/.local/bin/'
  ln="${body[$idx]}"
  if [[ "$ln" != "$exec_prefix"*'" "$@"' ]]; then
    printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return
  fi
  local rest="${ln#"$exec_prefix"}"
  local target="${rest%'" "$@"'}"
  [[ "$target" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]] || { printf 'no\t\t\t\n'; return; }

  printf 'ok\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$target" "$suffix" "$secrets"
}

# ── both directions of a set difference, reported ─────────────────────────
# `cross_check`, lifted out of `ccrc-adopt` and generalised by one step: the
# ENGINE (who is in A and not in B, and who is in B and not in A) lives here
# once; the WORDING stays with each caller, because the two callers are saying
# genuinely different things about the same arithmetic. Adopt reports three
# independent sources that usually agree with its discovery; doctor reports a
# roster and a disk that must agree. Both obey the same rule, which is the
# whole point of the shape: REPORT the disagreement, never resolve it by
# silently trusting one side (ccrc-adopt:41-48, "the direction of every
# uncertain call is to report it").
#
#   _wrap_set_diff <callback> <name of array A> <name of array B>
#
# calls `<callback> a-only <item>` for each member of A missing from B, in A's
# order, and THEN `<callback> b-only <item>` for each member of B missing from
# A. That order is adopt's original output order and is part of the contract.
#
# The callback sees the CALLER's locals (bash's dynamic scope), which is how
# doctor's callback appends to the finding list `_check_wrappers` declared —
# no globals, no output parsing.
#
# The two arrays travel BY NAME (`local -n`) rather than as one flattened
# argument list, because a flattened list cannot express "A is empty": adopt's
# original signature took `<label> <all of A...>` and worked only because it
# closed over B. A caller must therefore not name its arrays `_wrap_diff_a` or
# `_wrap_diff_b`, which bash would reject as a circular reference.
#
# EVERY LOCAL HERE IS `_wrap_*`-PREFIXED, and that is a correctness rule rather
# than tidiness (Task 5 review, Minor 4): the callback runs INSIDE this
# function's scope, so a bare `local x` here would shadow the caller's own `x`
# for the duration of every callback — the same dynamic scope this design
# relies on to hand the callback its caller's arrays would also hand it the
# wrong `x`. The reserved names are therefore all of `_wrap_cb`,
# `_wrap_diff_a`, `_wrap_diff_b`, `_wrap_x`, `_wrap_y`, `_wrap_found`, and a
# callback must not read a variable of any of those names expecting its own.
_wrap_set_diff() {
  local _wrap_cb="$1"
  local -n _wrap_diff_a="$2"
  local -n _wrap_diff_b="$3"
  local _wrap_x _wrap_y _wrap_found
  for _wrap_x in ${_wrap_diff_a[@]+"${_wrap_diff_a[@]}"}; do
    _wrap_found=0
    for _wrap_y in ${_wrap_diff_b[@]+"${_wrap_diff_b[@]}"}; do
      [ "$_wrap_y" = "$_wrap_x" ] && { _wrap_found=1; break; }
    done
    [ "$_wrap_found" -eq 1 ] || "$_wrap_cb" a-only "$_wrap_x"
  done
  for _wrap_x in ${_wrap_diff_b[@]+"${_wrap_diff_b[@]}"}; do
    _wrap_found=0
    for _wrap_y in ${_wrap_diff_a[@]+"${_wrap_diff_a[@]}"}; do
      [ "$_wrap_y" = "$_wrap_x" ] && { _wrap_found=1; break; }
    done
    [ "$_wrap_found" -eq 1 ] || "$_wrap_cb" b-only "$_wrap_x"
  done
}
